<ServiceLayout
  title="Estate Planning for Young Adults | Just Starting Out | Mesa Estate Planning"
  description="Turning 18 means your parents can no longer make medical or financial decisions for you. A healthcare directive and power of attorney are the essential starting point. Mesa, AZ estate planning attorney."
  heroTitle="You're 18. Your parents can no longer make decisions for you."
  heroSubtitle="Without a healthcare directive and power of attorney, no one can — not even in an emergency. These two documents take less than an hour and cost less than you think."
  breadcrumbParent="Life Stages"
  breadcrumbParentUrl="/life-stages"
  keywords="estate planning young adults, estate planning 18 year old, healthcare directive arizona, power of attorney young adult, estate planning college student"
  relatedServices={[
    { title: 'Healthcare Directive', href: '/services/healthcare-directive', desc: 'Name a healthcare agent and document your medical wishes.' },
    { title: 'Financial Power of Attorney', href: '/services/financial-power-of-attorney', desc: 'Authorize someone to handle finances if you can\'t.' },
    { title: 'Wills & Trusts', href: '/services/wills-and-trusts', desc: 'Basic will planning for single adults with assets.' },
  ]}
>
  <div class="quick-answer">
    <div class="quick-answer-label">Quick Answer</div>
    <p>At 18, you are a legal adult. HIPAA prevents your doctors from sharing medical information with your parents without your written authorization. Arizona law does not allow anyone — including your parents — to make financial or healthcare decisions for you unless you have signed documents granting that authority. A <a href="/services/healthcare-directive">healthcare directive</a> and <a href="/services/financial-power-of-attorney">financial power of attorney</a> are the two documents every adult in Arizona should have, regardless of age or wealth.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="content-section">
    <p>Most people associate estate planning with older adults, significant wealth, or complicated family situations. That association causes millions of young adults to skip the most basic legal protections — the ones they're most likely to actually need.</p>

    <p>If you're in a car accident and can't communicate, who talks to your doctors? If you're studying abroad and a bank account needs to be managed, who has legal authority? If you're 22 and rent an apartment, own a car, and have a retirement account through your employer — those are assets, and without any planning at all, Arizona's intestacy laws decide who gets them.</p>

    <p>The documents are simple. The conversation is short. The cost is minimal. The risk of not having them is not.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="content-section">
    <h2>What You Need at This Stage</h2>
    <p><strong>Healthcare Directive (Medical Power of Attorney + Living Will).</strong> Names someone — typically a parent, sibling, or trusted friend — to make medical decisions if you can't. Also records your wishes about life-sustaining treatment. Under Arizona law (A.R.S. § 36-3221), this can be a single document that covers both your healthcare agent designation and your treatment preferences.</p>

    <p><strong>Financial Power of Attorney.</strong> Authorizes someone to manage your finances — pay bills, access bank accounts, handle insurance claims — if you're incapacitated. Without this, your family would need to petition an Arizona court for a conservatorship, which is expensive, time-consuming, and public.</p>

    <p><strong>HIPAA Authorization.</strong> Allows named individuals to access your medical records and speak with your healthcare providers. Even with a healthcare directive, a separate HIPAA release ensures your family can get information quickly in an emergency.</p>

    <p><strong>Basic Will (if you have assets).</strong> If you own a car, have a bank account, or have a retirement plan through your employer, a simple <a href="/services/wills-and-trusts">will</a> ensures those assets go where you want them — not where Arizona default law sends them.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="content-section">
    <h2>Common Situations We See</h2>
    <p><strong>College students leaving home.</strong> Parents are often surprised to learn they have no legal authority over their 18-year-old's medical or financial decisions. A healthcare directive and HIPAA authorization, signed before the student leaves for school, close that gap completely.</p>

    <p><strong>Young professionals with employer benefits.</strong> A 401(k), employer life insurance, and an HSA are all assets with beneficiary designations. If you haven't updated them — or never designated a beneficiary at all — the defaults may not match your wishes.</p>

    <p><strong>Digital assets.</strong> Cryptocurrency, online banking, investment apps, social media accounts — if no one knows your passwords and no one has legal authority to access your accounts, those assets can be effectively lost. A power of attorney and a basic digital asset plan address this.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="content-section">
    <h2>What It Costs</h2>
    <p>Planning at this stage is the most affordable estate planning we do. Everything is flat-fee, quoted after a <a href="/contact">free consultation</a>. Most young adults leave with the documents they need in a single appointment.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="content-section">
    <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
    <div class="faq-section">
      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-btn" onclick="toggleFaq(this)">
          I'm healthy and don't have much money. Do I really need an estate plan?
          <span class="faq-icon">+</span>
        </button>
        <div class="faq-answer">
          <p>Yes. Estate planning at this stage isn't about wealth — it's about decision-making authority. Without a healthcare directive, no one can authorize emergency surgery for you. Without a power of attorney, no one can manage your bank account if you're incapacitated. These documents exist for exactly the situation where you can't speak for yourself.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-btn" onclick="toggleFaq(this)">
          Can my parents still make decisions for me after I turn 18?
          <span class="faq-icon">+</span>
        </button>
        <div class="faq-answer">
          <p>No. Once you turn 18, you are a legal adult in Arizona. HIPAA prevents your doctors from sharing your medical information with anyone — including your parents — without your written consent. Your parents have no legal authority to manage your finances, access your bank accounts, or make healthcare decisions for you unless you have signed documents granting that authority.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-btn" onclick="toggleFaq(this)">
          What if I'm a college student living in another state?
          <span class="faq-icon">+</span>
        </button>
        <div class="faq-answer">
          <p>If you're an Arizona resident attending school in another state, Arizona documents are generally recognized. However, having the documents is what matters most — in an emergency, a hospital needs to see a signed healthcare directive naming your agent, regardless of which state issued it. We prepare documents that work across state lines.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-btn" onclick="toggleFaq(this)">
          How long does this take?
          <span class="faq-icon">+</span>
        </button>
        <div class="faq-answer">
          <p>For a young adult with straightforward needs — healthcare directive, financial power of attorney, HIPAA authorization — the process typically involves one consultation and one signing appointment. Most clients complete everything within a week or two.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <script>
    function toggleFaq(btn) {
      const item = btn.closest('.faq-item');
      const isOpen = item.classList.contains('open');
      document.querySelectorAll('.faq-item.open').forEach(el => el.classList.remove('open'));
      if (!isOpen) item.classList.add('open');
    }
  </script>
</ServiceLayout>
